## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its native security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto). .NET on macOS delegates to OS-provided crypto, so once Apple dropped it, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. This was a known upcoming breaking change that landed in .NET 11.

Your code still works on Linux and Windows because those platforms still provide DSA via OpenSSL and Windows CNG respectively.

## How to fix it

**Option A — Migrate away from DSA (recommended)**
DSA is a legacy algorithm. If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**.

**Option B — Use OpenSSL on macOS for legacy verification**
If you must verify existing DSA signatures, you can P/Invoke or use a managed/OpenSSL-backed implementation. Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and use `SafeEvpPKeyHandle.OpenPublicKeyFromEngine` or a library like **Portable.BouncyCastle**:

```csharp
// NuGet: Portable.BouncyCastle
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] data, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    signer.Init(false, keyParam);
    signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is a pure managed implementation — works on all platforms with no OS crypto dependency.

**Option C — Runtime guard**
If DSA is optional, guard the call:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
```

**TL;DR:** Apple killed DSA → .NET followed suit on macOS. Use BouncyCastle for cross-platform legacy verification, or migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519.
